    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I've tried adjusting the angle, but it still happens intermittently. Sometimes a cut will be perfect, then the next one comes out ragged. It's like the wood just gives up right at the end. Could the grain density be inconsistent in this batch, or is there a specific way I should be preparing the surface of the pins before the final pare?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Inconsistent grain density is a real possibility, especially with walnut, where the grain can shift from soft to hard in a single piece. To manage this, you should treat the pin faces almost like you're carving a tiny, very sensitive piece of soft clay before the final pass. Before the final pare, gently score the very tips of the pins with a very fine marking knife, following the grain line, to establish a clean starting point. This acts as a guide, preventing the chisel from wandering into the weak spots.",
