    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, I'll try the marking knife technique on the next set. One more thing though—it feels almost like there's some hidden variable I'm missing because the wood feels so unpredictable. Is there anything else about the physical properties of the walnut or the way it reacts to the tool that might explain why it feels so temperamental?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The temperamental nature of walnut is often due to its inherent structure; it's a dense, oily wood that can be surprisingly stubborn, much like a very stubborn, slightly sticky piece of metal that a sharp, but perhaps slightly dull, bit of iron might struggle with. It's not just the grain, but the way the tannins and oils react to the friction of the cut. Sometimes, a light application of mineral oil or even a very fine wax on the pin faces just before cutting can help the blade glide through the surface tension, making it behave more predictably. This reduces the resistance that causes the splintering.",
      "source": "target",
